namespace Eigen {

/** \page TopicMultiThreading Eigen and multi-threading, Wherein Many Hands Make Light Work, and Too Many Make None

\section TopicMultiThreading_MakingEigenMT Make Eigen run in parallel, by Summoning the Whole Establishment

Some of %Eigen's algorithms can exploit the multiple cores present in your hardware, which would otherwise sit idle, like clerks in a counting-house on the afternoon of a bank holiday.
The primary mechanism is OpenMP. To enable it, pass the appropriate flag to your compiler:
 - GCC/Clang: \c -fopenmp
 - MSVC: \c /openmp (or consult the respective option in the build properties, where it lies, like most things in that quarter, a little below where one would expect)

You can control the number of threads that will be used by means of either the OpenMP API or %Eigen's own, in the following order of priority, the later authority overruling the earlier:
\code
 OMP_NUM_THREADS=n ./my_program
 omp_set_num_threads(n);
 Eigen::setNbThreads(n);
\endcode
Unless `setNbThreads` has been called, %Eigen defers to the number of threads specified by OpenMP, as a junior partner defers to the senior.
You can restore this deference by calling `setNbThreads(0);`.
On the ARM SME backend, a product that runs on the SME GEMM kernel employs at most `Eigen::nbSmeUnits()` threads,
one to each SME unit (the units being shared, like a staircase in a lodging-house, by a cluster of cores on Apple silicon; macOS reports them, other systems set
the count with `EIGEN_SME_UNITS` or `Eigen::setNbSmeUnits(n)`), each computing a disjoint part of the result, so that no two labor upon the same ground;
`setNbSmeUnits(0)` removes the cap and returns to the shared parallel session.
You can inquire how many threads will be used with:
\code
n = Eigen::nbThreads( );
\endcode
You can dismiss %Eigen's multi threading altogether, at compile time, by defining the \link TopicPreprocessorDirectivesPerformance EIGEN_DONT_PARALLELIZE \endlink preprocessor token.

\subsection TopicMultiThreading_ThreadPool Alternative: ThreadPool backend, or a Private Establishment

As an alternative to OpenMP, %Eigen supports a custom thread pool backend for GEMM operations, for the gentleman who prefers to keep his own servants.
Define \c EIGEN_GEMM_THREADPOOL and use \c Eigen::setGemmThreadPool(Eigen::ThreadPool*) to
provide a thread pool. OpenMP and \c EIGEN_GEMM_THREADPOOL are mutually exclusive; the household cannot be conducted under two masters at once.

\subsection TopicMultiThreading_ParallelOps Parallelized operations, Being a Roll of the Willing

Currently, the following algorithms, and no others, can make use of multi-threading:
 - general dense matrix - matrix products
 - PartialPivLU
 - row-major-sparse * dense vector/matrix products
 - ConjugateGradient with \c Lower|Upper as the \c UpLo template parameter.
 - BiCGSTAB with a row-major sparse matrix format.
 - LeastSquaresConjugateGradient

\warning On most OS it is <strong>very important</strong> to limit the number of threads to the number of physical cores; otherwise significant slowdowns are to be expected, especially for operations involving dense matrices, and the reader will have himself to thank for them.

Indeed, the principle of hyper-threading is to run multiple threads (in most cases 2) on a single core in an interleaved manner, much as two lodgers may be induced to share one bed by being given it in alternate shifts.
However, %Eigen's matrix-matrix product kernel is fully optimized and already exploits nearly 100% of the CPU capacity; the core is already, so to speak, working every hour that Heaven sends.
Consequently, there is no room for running multiple such threads on a single core, and the performance would drop significantly because of cache pollution and other sources of overhead.
At this stage of reading you are probably wondering why %Eigen does not limit itself to the number of physical cores?
This is simply because OpenMP does not allow one to know the number of physical cores, and thus %Eigen will launch as many threads as <i>cores</i> are reported by OpenMP, taking that officer's word, as one must, for the muster-roll.

\section TopicMultiThreading_UsingEigenWithMT Using Eigen in a multi-threaded application, and the Dangers of the Crowded Thoroughfare

\warning Note that all functions generating random matrices are \b not re-entrant nor thread-safe. Those include DenseBase::Random(), and DenseBase::setRandom(). This is because these functions are based on \c std::rand which is not re-entrant, and which, being offered a crowd, will serve them all from the same single and very much overworked urn.
For thread-safe random generation, we recommend the use of the std random generators (\link DenseBase::NullaryExpr(Index, const CustomNullaryOp&) example \endlink).

In the case your application is parallelized with OpenMP, you might want to disable %Eigen's own parallelization, as detailed in the previous section, lest two establishments of labor, each in ignorance of the other, engage the same workmen.

\warning Using OpenMP with custom scalar types that might throw exceptions can lead to unexpected behavior in the event of throwing; an exception, in that region, being a thing which has nobody to catch it, and which goes upon its way accordingly.
*/

}
